-- Q6041: Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 469(d)(2), passive activity credits may only offset tax attributable to passive income. Callahan''s $14,000 of credits is limited to $8,000 (the tax on passive income), and the remaining $6,000 is carried forward to future years when sufficient passive income tax liability exists.
Wrong (A): Passive credits cannot be applied against total tax liability; they are limited to the tax attributable to net passive income under Section 469(d)(2).
Wrong (B): Suspended passive credits are not permanently lost. Like passive losses, they carry forward indefinitely until utilized or the activity is disposed of.
Wrong (D): The IRC does not provide a mechanism to convert unused passive credits into passive loss deductions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 6041;

-- Q6042: Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 469(d)(1), passive losses may only be deducted against passive income. Currently, the car wash''s $20,000 of passive income partially offsets the $50,000 combined passive losses, suspending $30,000. If Reeves achieves material participation, the car wash becomes nonpassive, removing $20,000 from the passive pool and increasing suspended losses to $50,000 annually.
Wrong (A): Reclassification matters because it removes $20,000 of passive income from the netting pool, increasing the net suspended passive loss.
Wrong (C): Reclassifying the car wash as nonpassive does not affect self-employment tax treatment, which depends on the entity structure and type of income.
Wrong (D): Passive losses cannot offset active or nonpassive bookstore income. Removing the passive income source makes the loss situation worse, not better.$EXPL$
